Task 1: Creating Users in IdM
Subtask 1.1: Understanding the ipa user-add Command
The ipa user-add command is the primary tool for creating new users in IdM. This command allows you to specify various user attributes during creation.

Basic syntax:

ipa user-add [username] --first=[firstname] --last=[lastname] --email=[email]
Subtask 1.2: Creating Your First User
Let's create a user named john with complete information:

Connect to your IdM server (if not already connected):
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted.

Create the first user:
ipa user-add john --first=John --last=Doe --email=john.doe@example.com --password
When prompted, enter a temporary password for the user (e.g., TempPass123!)

Verify the user creation:

ipa user-show john
Subtask 1.3: Creating Multiple Users with Different Attributes
Now let's create several more users to work with:

Create a user with additional information:
ipa user-add jane --first=Jane --last=Smith --email=jane.smith@example.com --title="Software Engineer" --phone=555-0123 --password
Create a user for the IT department:
ipa user-add mike --first=Mike --last=Johnson --email=mike.johnson@example.com --title="System Administrator" --department="IT" --password
Create a user for the HR department:
ipa user-add sarah --first=Sarah --last=Wilson --email=sarah.wilson@example.com --title="HR Manager" --department="Human Resources" --password
Subtask 1.4: Understanding User Attributes
Each user in IdM can have various attributes. Here are the most commonly used ones:

--first: First name
--last: Last name
--email: Email address
--title: Job title
--department: Department name
--phone: Phone number
--manager: Manager's username
--password: Prompts for password creation
Task 2: Creating and Managing Groups
Subtask 2.1: Understanding Groups in IdM
Groups in IdM help organize users and assign permissions collectively. There are two types of groups:

POSIX groups: Traditional Unix groups with GID numbers
Non-POSIX groups: Used primarily for access control
Subtask 2.2: Creating Groups
Create an IT department group:
ipa group-add it-department --desc="IT Department Staff"
Create an HR department group:
ipa group-add hr-department --desc="Human Resources Department"
Create a developers group:
ipa group-add developers --desc="Software Development Team"
Create a managers group:
ipa group-add managers --desc="Management Team"
Subtask 2.3: Adding Users to Groups
Now let's assign users to appropriate groups using the ipa group-add-member command:

Add Mike to the IT department group:
ipa group-add-member it-department --users=mike
Add Sarah to the HR department group:
ipa group-add-member hr-department --users=sarah
Add John and Jane to the developers group:
ipa group-add-member developers --users=john,jane
Add Sarah to the managers group (users can belong to multiple groups):
ipa group-add-member managers --users=sarah
Subtask 2.4: Verifying Group Memberships
Check the IT department group members:
ipa group-show it-department
Check all groups that John belongs to:
ipa user-show john --all
Task 3: Finding and Listing Users and Groups
Subtask 3.1: Using ipa user-find to Search Users
The ipa user-find command is powerful for searching and filtering users:

List all users:
ipa user-find
Find users by first name:
ipa user-find --first=John
Find users by department:
ipa user-find --department=IT
Find users by email domain:
ipa user-find --email=*@example.com
Subtask 3.2: Advanced User Searches
Find users with specific job titles:
ipa user-find --title="*Engineer*"
Find users and display specific attributes only:
ipa user-find --all --raw
Find users created recently (useful for auditing):
ipa user-find --all | grep -A 10 -B 2 "$(date +%Y)"
Subtask 3.3: Finding and Listing Groups
List all groups:
ipa group-find
Find groups by description:
ipa group-find --desc="*Department*"
Show detailed information about a specific group:
ipa group-show developers --all
Subtask 3.4: Creating Useful Reports
Create a user summary report:
echo "=== User Summary Report ===" > user_report.txt
echo "Total Users: $(ipa user-find | grep "Number of entries" | cut -d: -f2)" >> user_report.txt
echo "" >> user_report.txt
echo "Users by Department:" >> user_report.txt
ipa user-find --department=IT | grep "User login" >> user_report.txt
ipa user-find --department="Human Resources" | grep "User login" >> user_report.txt
View the report:
cat user_report.txt
Advanced Operations
Managing User Properties
Modify user information:
ipa user-mod john --title="Senior Software Engineer"
Add a manager to a user:
ipa user-mod jane --manager=sarah
Group Management Operations
Remove a user from a group:
ipa group-remove-member developers --users=john
Add the user back to the group:
ipa group-add-member developers --users=john
Bulk Operations
For managing many users, you can create scripts:

#!/bin/bash
# Create multiple users from a list
users=("alice:Alice:Brown" "bob:Bob:Green" "charlie:Charlie:White")

for user_info in "${users[@]}"; do
    IFS=':' read -r username firstname lastname <<< "$user_info"
    ipa user-add "$username" --first="$firstname" --last="$lastname" --email="${username}@example.com" --password
done
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Authentication Problems
If you encounter authentication errors:

# Renew your Kerberos ticket
kinit admin
# Check ticket status
klist
Issue 2: User Already Exists
If you try to create a user that already exists:

# Check if user exists first
ipa user-find --login=username
# If needed, delete and recreate
ipa user-del username
Issue 3: Group Membership Issues
If group operations fail:

# Verify group exists
ipa group-show groupname
# Check current memberships
ipa group-show groupname --all
Security Best Practices
Always use strong temporary passwords when creating users
Force users to change passwords on first login
Regularly audit group memberships
Use descriptive group names and descriptions
Follow your organization's naming conventions
